{"id": "f7a78158-9c1b-4502-9b66-9d883dab11b4", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T05:52:26.587835", "source": "environment", "parent_id": "4026a90d-72a9-48d5-a8b9-92e999e16106", "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "call-ae5e0fa9-6149-4b3b-b276-af819c785a45", "observation": {"content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"phase\": \"exploration\", \"command\": \"find /workspace -type f 2>/dev/null; echo \\\"---\\\"; find / -maxdepth 3 -name \\\"track.md\\\" 2>/dev/null; echo \\\"---\\\"; find / -maxdepth 3 -name \\\"README*\\\" 2>/dev/null | head -20\", \"exit_code\": 0, \"timed_out\": false, \"elapsed_seconds\": 0.032, \"output\": \"/workspace/task.json\\n---\\n---\\n/lib/ispell/README.select-ispell\\n\", \"output_truncated\": false}"}], "is_error": false, "kind": "BenchmarkObservation"}, "action_id": "a074b4c1-41cd-4d41-a83e-8bb305f7cdd2", "extended_content": [], "kind": "ObservationEvent", "source_event_index": 9}
{"id": "42e60a2f-6211-4aab-b288-e0b5d3793385", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T05:53:22.094332", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "f7a78158-9c1b-4502-9b66-9d883dab11b4", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-bd3ec497-4d19-4f2f-93f3-b62ad3f18b7a", "tool_call": {"id": "call-bd3ec497-4d19-4f2f-93f3-b62ad3f18b7a", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Initial inspection + proposed approach plan\",\"stage\":\"plan\",\"answer\":\"## 1. Inspection findings (evidence)\\n\\n`/workspace` contains **only** `task.json`:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"question\\\": \\\"How does the performance of a small LM (e.g., GPT-2) compare to a larger\\n   code-specialized LM (e.g., CodeLlama-7b) on the RepoBench Python dataset\\n   for the 'cross_file_first' setting?\\\",\\n  \\\"context\\\": \\\"Design an experiment that answers the question. ... Do not claim that\\n   an experiment was run.\\\",\\n  \\\"documents\\\": { \\\"track.md\\\": \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\" }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nConcretely observed: no `README*`, no `track.md` outside the JSON reference, no datasets, no model weights, no source code in `/workspace`; runtime has 24 CPU cores, **no GPU**, **network disabled**, no Python deps preinstalled. Therefore the deliverable is a **paper design only**; no execution is claimed.\\n\\nRepoBench background I am recalling (NOT source-verified here; flagged as recalled):\\n- RepoBench (Bairi et al., 2023, arXiv:2306.03091) was a Py150-based repository-level Python completion benchmark retiring completion scenarios of \\\"in-file\\\" vs \\\"cross-file\\\" prompting with two context-ordering configurations; **`cross_file_first`** refers to the configuration where cross-file retrieved snippets are placed **before** the in-file prefix in the prompt (paired with `cross_file_last`).\\n- Reported metrics in the original paper are **Edit Similarity (ES, character-level, CodeXGLUE-style)** as primary and **Exact Match (EM)** as secondary; some follow-up revisions swap primary to identifier-level F1.\\n- Subset construction: RepoBench typically reports on a **random** split and a **hard** split (the latter requires a retrieval-threshold filter); a \\\"next-line\\\" granularity is the canonical single-line target, distinct from a \\\"next-chunk\\\" longer target.\\n\\nPlease confirm/deny these recalled facts in your next message.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Proposed approach (compact)\\n\\n### 2.1 RQ & hypotheses\\n- **RQ:** Does a small non-code LM (GPT-2 family) match, fall behind, or exceed a 7B code-specialized LM (CodeLlama-7b) on RepoBench Python `cross_file_first` repository-level next-line completion, under matched context and decoding budget?\\n- **H1:** CodeLlama-7b > GPT-2 family on both ES and EM.\\n- **H0:** No difference; rejection threshold α = 0.05 via paired bootstrap CI on per-instance deltas.\\n\\n### 2.2 Independent variables\\n| Factor | Levels |\\n|---|---|\\n| Model family | GPT-2 (small 124M primary; medium 355M as size-parity sensor) |\\n| Model specialization | CodeLlama-7b-Python (primary, code-specialized); CodeLlama-7b base (specialization ablation) |\\n| Prompt mode | Code-as-natural-language (both); FIM (CodeLlama only — GPT-2 not FIM-capable) |\\n| Decoding temp | 0.0 (greedy, primary); 0.2 / 0.8 (ES sensitivity only) |\\n\\n### 2.3 Dependent variables (FLAGGED ASSUMPTION — confirm metric)\\n- **ES** (primary): CodeXGLUE-style character-level edit similarity, range 0–100.\\n- **EM** (secondary): binary, lenient on whitespace, per-instance.\\n- Identifier-F1 reported only if you confirm it as the standard target.\\n- Instances yielding empty, out-of-context, or OOD generations → scored 0.\\n\\n### 2.4 Dataset & sampling\\n- RepoBench Python `cross_file_first` split (random split primary; hard split secondary if budget allows).\\n- Sample size: **N = 1,000** for primary paired analysis (Bairi-style \\\"1k-sample is informative\\\"); **N = 100** for reduced-budget variant.\\n- Sampling: deterministic, fixed seed 42, **stratified by repository** to avoid over-representation of any single repo; pairs are **same prompt → two models** (paired design).\\n\\n### 2.5 Prompting (KEY ASSUMPTION)\\n- **Mode A (primary, paired across both models):** concatenate `[system instruction] + [cross-file retrieved snippets, file-path-header] + [in-file prefix up to cursor]` and let the model continue; predict first emitted line.\\n- **Mode B (FIM, CodeLlama only):** `<PRE> + in-file prefix + <SUF> + cross-file snippets + <MID>` per CodeLlama FIM spec; never paired against GPT-2.\\n- Max context: enforce the same per-instance truncation rule across models (last-N-tokens tie-breaker) so pairing remains valid.\\n- Special considerations: GPT-2 vocab/BPE mismatches with Python keywords — not a problem for ES (char-level), only for any byte-level back-translation; flag.\\n\\n### 2.6 Generation config\\n| Setting | Value |\\n|---|---|\\n| Temperature | 0.0 (greedy, primary) + 0.2, 0.8 (sensitivity) |\\n| top_p | 0.95 (non-greedy only) |\\n| max_new_tokens | 128 (line + buffer); truncate to predicted single line for EM scoring |\\n| Batch | dynamic padding; per-model mini-batch sized to compute budget |\\n| n samples per instance | 1 |\\n\\n### 2.7 Controls / baselines\\n- **Majority-class baseline:** predict most-frequent next line in train split.\\n- **Copy-prefix baseline:** echo last in-file non-empty line.\\n- **Copy-import baseline:** surface first imported identifier (sanity check on retrieval usage).\\n- **Oracle ceiling:** score the gold next line against itself = 100 (trivial sanity).\\n- **Random-token baseline:** uniform sample (lower-bound sanity).\\n- **Model size parity post-hoc:** plot ES vs log10(params) adding medium/large/XL.\\n\\n### 2.8 Evaluation pipeline\\n- Normalization: trim whitespace, collapse repeated spaces, preserve identifiers & string content case; do NOT strip comments unless gold also strips.\\n- ES: character-level Levenshtein (LCS-normalised) per CodeXGLUE convention; **flag** if you want token-merge edits instead.\\n- EM: exact string match after normalization, binary.\\n- Out-of-context / empty / OOD generations → ES=0, EM=0; counted in denominator.\\n- Per-repo aggregation to detect clustering effects.\\n\\n### 2.9 Statistical analysis\\n- **Primary:** paired non-parametric bootstrap (B = 10,000, seed 42) on per-instance Δ(ES) and Δ(EM) between the two arms; percentile CI.\\n- Effect size: Cohen's d (paired) for ES; Cohen's h for EM.\\n- Multi-comparison correction: Holm-Bonferroni within the model-family family (if > 2 variants reported). Single primary contrast carries no correction.\\n- Sensitivity: random vs hard split, context-length deciles, per-repo median.\\n\\n### 2.10 Feasibility (no-GPU, CPU-only, network off)\\n| Item | Estimate |\\n|---|---|\\n| GPT-2 small (CPU fp32) | minutes per 1k instances → tractable |\\n| CodeLlama-7b (CPU, INT4 GPTQ/AWQ) | ~0.5–2 tok/s/thread → 1k × 128 tok / 24 threads / 0.5 efficiency ≈ 30–60 min |\\n| CodeLlama-7b (CPU, fp16) | infeasible without GPU |\\n| Dependencies (`transformers`, `torch`, `datasets`, `scipy`, ES impl) | **not** preinstalled; assumed pip-installable in design only |\\n| Dataset | not bundled; would need pre-packaged HF/parquet load via local path under network-disabled mode |\\n\\n**Reduced-budget variant (recommended for paper design):** N = 100, greedy decode, INT4 CodeLlama, GPT-2 small only, drop Mode B, drop sensitivity temperature sweep, drop random/hard split contrast → ~5–10 min total on this 24-CPU machine in principle, but no execution performed or claimed.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Uncertainties & assumptions requiring your confirmation\\n\\n1. **GPT-2 size** — small (124M) is the canonical \\\"small\\\"; brief silent.\\n2. **CodeLlama-7b variant** — base vs Python vs Instruct (recommend Python for \\\"code-specialized\\\").\\n3. **`cross_file_first` definition** — confirmed in my recollection as **context-ordering** (cross-file snippets prepended); please verify.\\n4. **Primary metric** — ES (CodeXGLUE) primary, EM secondary per the original paper; some revisions use Identifier-F1 — please confirm.\\n5. **Target granularity** — next-line (likely default) vs next-chunk — please confirm.\\n6. **Random vs hard split** — both exist in the dataset; primary endpoint needs fixing.\\n7. **FIM mode in CodeLlama** — CodeLlama supports FIM with `<FILL_ME>` markers; pairing against GPT-2 is invalid; confirm whether to report Mode B as a CodeLlama-only arm.\\n8. **ES tokenization convention** — character-level Levenshtein vs token-merge edits — choose one.\\n9. **Truncation rule** — \\\"last `min(model_window, max_prompt_tokens)` tokens\\\" proposed; choose.\\n10. **Dataset licensing/local packaging** — RepoBench data is not present locally; design assumes pre-packaged local copy under `data/repo_bench/`; network off means online fetch is not part of the design.\\n11. **Identifier-F1 inclusion** — report or skip?\\n12. **Statistical unit** — I propose per-instance paired tests as primary with per-repo sensitivity; please confirm.\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Awaiting your approval or revisions before producing the full design (stage=result).**\",\"evidence\":\"Only file in /workspace is /workspace/task.json (verified via `ls -la /workspace` and `inspect_repository /workspace/task.json`). `find / -maxdepth 3 -name \\\"track.md\\\"` returns nothing outside the JSON reference. `find / -maxdepth 3 -name \\\"README*\\\"` finds only `/lib/ispell/README.select-ispell` — i.e., no project README. Root listing shows /workspace, /home, /tmp, /etc among writable dirs; no datasets, no source code or model artifacts anywhere in /workspace. Recalled RepoBench facts (metric = ES + EM, cross_file_first = context-ordering variant, splits = random/hard) come from background knowledge of Bairi et al. 2023 (arXiv:2306.03091) and are NOT source-verified inside this workspace — flagged in the answer.\",\"assumptions\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"FIM mode is supported by CodeLlama and is reported as a CodeLlama-only arm, never paired against GPT-2.</item>\",\"$text\":\"Per-instance paired test is the primary statistical unit with per-repo sensitivity.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Python deps (`transformers`, `torch`, `datasets`, `scipy`) are not preinstalled but installable in a real environment (observed ls; not verified).</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"RepoBench dataset is available packaged locally or pre-vendored; not bundled in /workspace (observed; flagged).</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"CodeLlama-7b-Python is the appropriate \\\"code-specialized\\\" variant (recalled; flagged).</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"GPT-2 size = small (124M); alternative = medium/large/XL (recalled; flagged).</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"cross_file_first = context-ordering placing cross-file snippets before the in-file prefix (recalled; flagged).</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"ES is the primary metric, EM secondary, both per Bairi et al. 2023 (recalled; flagged).</item>\\n\"}],\"uncertainties\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"Decoding-budget policy: is greedy enough, or should the design mandate a small beam/T-sweep?</item>\",\"$text\":\"Whether dependency installation and any local vendoring of data weights are in scope for the design or must be assumed.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Whether to include a non-FIM Mode A *and* a FIM Mode B contrast, or only Mode A for a cleaner paired comparison.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Truncation rule when context exceeds model window — confirm \\\"last-N-tokens\\\" rule across both models.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"ES tokenization convention — character-level Levenshtein vs token-merge edits.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Prompt format and FIM marker syntax — confirm `print` template and exact concatenation order.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Random vs hard split — which is the primary endpoint?</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Target granularity — next-line vs next-chunk, or both?</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Primary metric — ES/EM (CodeXGLUE) vs Identifier-level F1 vs both?</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Exact semantics of \\\"cross_file_first\\\" in the supplied RepoBench version — context ordering, split name, or both?</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Which CodeLlama-7b variant — base, Python, or Instruct?</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Which GPT-2 size variant the brief intends — small, medium, large, or XL?</item>\\n\"}]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-26e52aab-3b61-41f3-a013-f344bf180280", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 10}
