{"id": "b6772c5f-e6c9-4777-ad24-abe1544f3736", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T05:53:49.054812", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "25bfeb9d-efb1-40d8-82cd-62044cace9a3", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-9d9e3a53-fd70-497b-9a78-0a5fe871b23e", "tool_call": {"id": "call-9d9e3a53-fd70-497b-9a78-0a5fe871b23e", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Initial inspection + proposed approach plan\",\"stage\":\"plan\",\"answer\":\"## 1. Inspection findings (evidence)\\n\\n`/workspace` contains **only** `task.json`:\\n\\n```\\n{\\n  \\\"question\\\": \\\"How does the performance of a small LM (e.g., GPT-2) compare\\n   to a larger code-specialized LM (e.g., CodeLlama-7b) on the RepoBench\\n   Python dataset for the 'cross_file_first' setting?\\\",\\n  \\\"context\\\": \\\"Design an experiment that answers the question. ... Do not claim\\n   that an experiment was run.\\\",\\n  \\\"documents\\\": { \\\"track.md\\\": \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\" }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nConcretely observed: no README, no track.md outside the JSON reference, no datasets, no model weights, no source code in /workspace; runtime has 24 CPU cores, **no GPU**, **network disabled**, no Python deps preinstalled. Therefore the deliverable is a **paper design only**; no execution is claimed.\\n\\nRepoBench background I am recalling (NOT source-verified here; flagged as recalled):\\n- RepoBench (Bairi et al., 2023, arXiv:2306.03091) is a Py150-based repository-level Python completion benchmark with \\\"in-file\\\" vs \\\"cross-file\\\" contexts and two orderings; `cross_file_first` is the configuration where cross-file retrieved snippets are placed **before** the in-file prefix (paired with `cross_file_last`).\\n- Reported metrics in the original paper are Edit Similarity (ES, character-level, CodeXGLUE-style) as primary and Exact Match (EM) as secondary; some follow-up revisions swap primary to identifier-level F1.\\n- Subset construction typically reports a random split and a \\\"hard\\\" split (retrieval-filter); next-line granularity is canonical, distinct from a \\\"next-chunk\\\" longer target.\\n\\nPlease confirm/deny these recalled facts in your next message.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Proposed approach (compact)\\n\\n### 2.1 RQ & hypotheses\\n- RQ: Does a small non-code LM (GPT-2 family) match, fall behind, or exceed a 7B code-specialized LM (CodeLlama-7b) on RepoBench Python cross_file_first repository-level next-line completion, under matched context and decoding budget?\\n- H1: CodeLlama-7b > GPT-2 family on both ES and EM.\\n- H0: No difference; rejection threshold alpha=0.05 via paired bootstrap CI on per-instance deltas.\\n\\n### 2.2 Independent variables\\n- Model family: GPT-2 (small 124M primary; medium 355M size-parity sensor)\\n- Specialization: CodeLlama-7b-Python (primary), CodeLlama-7b base (ablation)\\n- Prompt mode: code-as-natural-language (both); FIM (CodeLlama-only)\\n- Decoding temp: 0.0 (greedy) primary; 0.2 / 0.8 for ES sensitivity only\\n\\n### 2.3 Dependent variables (FLAGGED — confirm metric)\\n- ES (primary): CodeXGLUE-style character-level edit similarity, 0-100.\\n- EM (secondary): binary after normalization, per-instance.\\n- Identifier-F1 reported only if confirmed as standard target.\\n- Empty / out-of-context / OOD generations => scored 0.\\n\\n### 2.4 Dataset & sampling\\n- RepoBench Python cross_file_first split; random split primary, hard split secondary.\\n- N = 1000 primary paired analysis; N = 100 reduced-budget variant.\\n- Sampling: deterministic seed=42, stratified by repo to avoid over-representation; paired design (same prompt -> two models).\\n\\n### 2.5 Prompting (KEY ASSUMPTION)\\n- Mode A (primary, paired across both models): concatenate [system instruction] + [cross-file retrieved snippets with file-path header] + [in-file prefix up to cursor]; predict first emitted line.\\n- Mode B (FIM, CodeLlama-only): PRE/SUF markers per CodeLlama FIM spec; not paired against GPT-2.\\n- Same per-instance truncation rule across models to keep pairing valid.\\n\\n### 2.6 Generation config\\n- Temperature: 0.0 greedy primary; 0.2 and 0.8 sensitivity.\\n- top_p: 0.95 (non-greedy only).\\n- max_new_tokens: 128; truncate to predicted single line for EM scoring.\\n- n samples per instance: 1.\\n\\n### 2.7 Controls / baselines\\n- Majority-class baseline (most frequent next line in train).\\n- Copy-prefix baseline (echo last in-file line).\\n- Copy-import baseline (first imported identifier; sanity for retrieval dependence).\\n- Random-token baseline (lower-bound sanity).\\n- Oracle ceiling: gold next line vs itself = 100.\\n- Post-hoc size-parity plot: ES vs log10(params) across GPT-2 small/medium/large/XL where feasible.\\n\\n### 2.8 Evaluation pipeline\\n- Normalization: trim whitespace, collapse repeated spaces; preserve identifiers and string case; do not strip comments unless gold also strips.\\n- ES: character-level Levenshtein per CodeXGLUE convention (flag if token-merge edits preferred).\\n- EM: exact string match after normalization, binary.\\n- Out-of-context / empty / OOD => ES=0, EM=0.\\n- Per-repo aggregation to detect repo-level clustering.\\n\\n### 2.9 Statistical analysis\\n- Primary: paired non-parametric bootstrap (B=10000, seed=42) on per-instance delta ES and delta EM; percentile CI.\\n- Effect size: Cohen's d paired for ES; Cohen's h for EM.\\n- Multi-comparison: Holm-Bonferroni within the model-family family; single primary contrast needs no correction.\\n- Sensitivity: random-vs-hard split, context-length deciles, per-repo median.\\n\\n### 2.10 Feasibility (no-GPU, CPU-only, network off)\\n- GPT-2 small CPU fp32: minutes per 1k instances (tractable).\\n- CodeLlama-7b CPU INT4 (GPTQ/AWQ): ~0.5-2 tok/s/thread => 1k x 128 tok / 24 threads / 0.5 efficiency ~ 30-60 min.\\n- CodeLlama-7b CPU fp16: infeasible without GPU.\\n- Dependencies (transformers, torch, datasets, scipy, ES impl) NOT preinstalled; installable in a real environment only.\\n- Dataset not bundled; design assumes pre-packaged local copy under a network-disabled mode.\\n- Reduced-budget variant: N=100, greedy, INT4 CodeLlama, GPT-2 small only, drop Mode B and temp sweep => ~5-10 min in principle, but **no execution performed or claimed**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Uncertainties & assumptions requiring your confirmation\\n\\n1. GPT-2 size: small (124M) chosen as canonical; brief silent.\\n2. CodeLlama-7b variant: Python vs base vs Instruct; recommend Python for code-specialized arm.\\n3. cross_file_first semantics: confirmed in my recollection as context-ordering (cross-file snippets prepended); please verify.\\n4. Primary metric: ES (CodeXGLUE) primary, EM secondary; please confirm vs Identifier-F1.\\n5. Target granularity: next-line (likely default) vs next-chunk; please confirm.\\n6. Random vs hard split: which is the primary endpoint.\\n7. FIM arm: CodeLlama supports FIM with <FILL_ME> markers; confirm whether to report Mode B as CodeLlama-only.\\n8. ES tokenization: character-level Levenshtein vs token-merge edits; choose one.\\n9. Truncation rule: propose last-N-tokens rule applied equally to both arms.\\n10. Dataset licensing/local packaging: not present locally; design assumes pre-packaged copy.\\n11. Identifier-F1 inclusion: report or skip.\\n12. Statistical unit: per-instance paired primary, per-repo sensitivity; confirm.\\n\\nAwaiting your approval or revisions before producing the full design (stage=result).\",\"evidence\":\"Only file in /workspace is /workspace/task.json (verified via `ls -la /workspace` and `inspect_repository /workspace/task.json`). `find / -maxdepth 3 -name \\\"track.md\\\"` returns nothing outside the JSON reference. `find / -maxdepth 3 -name \\\"README*\\\"` finds only /lib/ispell/README.select-ispell — i.e., no project README. Root listing shows /workspace, /home, /tmp, /etc among writable dirs; no datasets, no source code or model artifacts anywhere in /workspace. Recalled RepoBench facts (metric=ES+EM, cross_file_first=context-ordering variant, splits=random/hard) come from background knowledge of Bairi et al. 2023 (arXiv:2306.03091) and are NOT source-verified inside this workspace — flagged in the answer.\",\"assumptions\":[{\"item\":[\"Python deps (transformers, torch, datasets, scipy) are not preinstalled but installable in a real environment (observed via ls; not verified).\",{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"FIM is supported by CodeLlama and reported as a CodeLlama-only arm, never paired against GPT-2.</item>\",\"$text\":\"ES is the primary metric, EM secondary, both per Bairi et al. 2023 (recalled; flagged).</item>\"},\"$text\":\"cross_file_first = context-ordering placing cross-file snippets before the in-file prefix (recalled; flagged).</item>\"},\"$text\":\"GPT-2 size = small (124M) primary; medium/large/XL as size-parity sensors (recalled; flagged).</item>\"},\"$text\":\"CodeLlama-7b-Python is the appropriate code-specialized variant (recalled; flagged).</item>\"},\"$text\":\"RepoBench dataset is assumed available as a packaged local copy; not bundled in /workspace (observed; flagged).</item>\"}],\"$text\":\"Per-instance paired test is the primary statistical unit, per-repo reported as sensitivity.</item>\"}],\"uncertainties\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"Which GPT-2 size variant the brief intends.</item>\",\"$text\":\"Which CodeLlama-7b variant.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Exact semantics of cross_file_first in the supplied RepoBench version.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Primary metric ES/EM vs Identifier-level F1 vs both.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Target granularity next-line vs next-chunk or both.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Random vs hard split primary endpoint.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Prompt format and FIM marker syntax exact concatenation order.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"ES tokenization convention character-level Levenshtein vs token-merge edits.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Truncation rule when context exceeds model window.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Whether to include a non-FIM Mode A and a FIM Mode B contrast, or only Mode A.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Whether dependency installation and local vendoring are in scope for the design.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Decoding-budget policy: greedy-only vs small temp sweep.</item>\"}]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-44cce93a-1f05-45d8-8390-e1a1fe1f20a4", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 12}
